  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1054 PM MDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nThis evening's H500 hand analysis map shows one system digging\nthrough the PacNW into the Northern Rockies with another on its\nheels just off the coast. These systems remain off to our North\nas they swing eastward the next few nights. Overall dendritic \nsaturation looks to remain outside our CWA though a few flurries\ncould be possible over the northern high mountains near the WY\nborder the next few nights. Dry weather continues through\nThursday before we begin to trend wetter as we head into the\nweekend. The high latitude pattern looks to fold over itself and\nclose a fairly strong low off the PacNW by the end of the week.\nThere is split flow over the West at this time with the strong \nPolar jet rounding the base of the PacNW low while the southern\nstream is directed across the Baja to the 4 Corners. This\nsoutherly stream will begin to pull SubTrop moisture back into\nour area by early Friday. A weak wave moving through the flow \nwill lead to scattered showers by the afternoon but overall QPF\namounts look to stay on the lighter side. The upstream split\nbreaks down as we go through the weekend and the resultant\nstronger upstream jet will force another highly anomalous plume\nof moisture into the Desert Southwest. PWAT is once again \n250-350 percent of normal as it moves into AZ early Sunday. How\nthe pattern ultimately develops Sunday into early next week \nwill have a big bearing on where the bulk of this moisture will \nbe transported. The consensus at the moment looks like the most \nanomalous mositure will remain mainly south of the 4 Corners but\nour southern CWA will once again get brushed with PWAT 200-250 \npercent of normal late Saturday into late Sunday. Saturated \nsoils from a very wet October and especially this last event \nwill make this a much more impactful forecast. Rainfall totals \ncould once again push streams back towards action stage. Snow \nlevels of 9500 to 10000 feet will once again keep the bulk of \nmid and lower elevation precipitation in the form of rainfall. \nStay tuned as forecasts become better refined.",
